  "comments": "<p>Despite being produced in their millions and being available at face value from most post offices and banks, the ordinary business strike was a Royal Mint commemorative issue and not intended for general circulation.&nbsp;</p><p><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/680799cbd8b22.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/680799cbd8b22.jpg\"></a><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/680799cb8678c.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/680799cb8678c.jpg\"></a></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The coin was also issued in an individual pack struck to the same quality, known as circulating standard, and was also released as part of a 12-coin set from The Royal Mint called Royal Wedding Crowns. The coin was also struck in silver proof.<br><br><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/60999158c5dec.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/60999158c5dec.jpg\" alt=\"\"></a></p>",
